      "almost_all_the_energy_becomes_photons_": " — almost all the energy becomes photons. This is why fireflies + bioluminescent organisms don't feel warm to the touch.",
      "ambush_hunters_lurking_in_crevices_the": "Ambush hunters lurking in crevices. They smell prey + strike from cover. Hiding in coral doesn't help — that's WHERE the eel is. Octopus must abandon shelter + flee.",
      "ambush_slow_stalk_strike": "🎭 Ambush — slow stalk + strike",
      "ammonite_fossil_suture_patterns_become": "Ammonite fossil suture patterns become more elaborate over evolutionary time — culminating in fractal-like designs in late Mesozoic species. The suture pattern probably reinforced the shell.",
      "ammonite_reverence": "Ammonite reverence",
      "ammonoid_cephalopods_dominated_the_oce": "Ammonoid cephalopods dominated the oceans for 300 million years before going extinct with the dinosaurs (~66 MYA). They left billions of fossils.",
      "ammonoids_diverge_from_nautiloids_dist": "Ammonoids diverge from nautiloids. Distinctive feature: complex suture lines where shell partitions meet the outer wall, creating zigzag patterns that strengthened the shell against pressure.",
      "among_the_most_venomous_animals_on_ear": "Among the most venomous animals on Earth. Sting causes cardiac arrest within minutes in humans. Australian box jellies kill more people per year than great white sharks. Have 24 eyes (16 simple + 8 image-forming) distributed on their bell.",
      "among_the_smallest_octopuses_subject_o": "Among the smallest octopuses. Subject of cognition + culture research.",
      "amphioctopus_marginatus_finn_et_al_200": "Amphioctopus marginatus, Finn et al. 2009.",
      "amphitretidae": "Amphitretidae",
      "an_abandoned_crevice_with_the_right_si": "An abandoned crevice with the right size for your body. Could be a safe rest spot. Smells okay (no predator scent).",
      "an_octopus_arm_has_thousands_of_sucker": "An octopus arm has thousands of suckers, each with up to 10,000 chemoreceptor cells. A single arm can taste more than your entire mouth + nose combined.",
      "an_octopus_has_a_beak_that_s_the_size_": "An octopus has a beak that's the size of its eye. It can squeeze through any opening bigger than the beak. What does this tell you about how its body is structured? Could you design a robot that does the same?",
      "an_octopus_mantle_can_contract_at_abou": "An octopus mantle can contract at about 0.2 m/s during a jet — by squid standards relatively slow. Squid mantle contraction during full jet can reach 0.8 m/s.",
      "an_octopus_the_size_of_a_grapefruit_ca": "An octopus the size of a grapefruit can squeeze through a hole the diameter of its eye (~2cm). The only rigid structure in the body is the chitinous beak.",
      "analysis": "📈 Analysis: ",
      "analysis_questions": "❓ Analysis questions",
      "analyze_data_to_support_the_claim_that": "Analyze data to support the claim that Newton's second law of motion describes the mathematical relationship among net force, mass, and acceleration.",
      "analyzed_50_years_of_fishery_ecosystem": ") analyzed 50+ years of fishery + ecosystem data across 35 species and found a consistent upward trend.",
      "analyzing_and_interpreting_data": "Analyzing and interpreting data",
      "anchor_in_time_space": "Anchor in time + space: ",
      "anchovy_populations_boom_bust_on_30_ye": "Anchovy populations boom + bust on ~30-year cycles tied to Pacific Decadal Oscillation. The boom years feed seabirds, marine mammals, AND fisheries. Octopus + squid populations track anchovy availability.",
      "ancient_lineage_still_evolving": "Ancient lineage, still evolving",
      "and_established_the_entire_framework_o": " and established the entire framework of modern neurophysiology. Every textbook diagram of an action potential, every sodium-channel-acting drug, every cardiology rhythm-control therapy, traces back to that squid.",
      "and_how_they": ", and how they ",
      "and_the_energy_released_comes_out_as_v": ", and the energy released comes out as visible light:",
